@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# Daily Grind security audit
+
+The original page had no JavaScript, event handlers, DOM injection sinks, forms,
+authentication logic, or third-party script dependencies. Its inline stylesheet
+and heading style prevented a policy that disallows inline CSS. Responses had no
+security headers. URL parsing also trusted the request's Host header and could
+throw on malformed input.
+
+The stylesheet now lives at `/styles.css`; the page's appearance and existing
+Unsplash image URL are preserved. The response handler serves only this explicit
+asset and the existing HTML route fallback, without exposing filesystem paths.
+It accepts GET/HEAD and handles malformed URLs without crashing.
+
+## Browser protections
+
+- CSP defaults to enforcement, blocks all scripts (including inline handlers),
+  plugins, framing, forms, base URL changes, and network APIs. Only same-origin
+  styles and images from `https://images.unsplash.com` are allowed. No wildcard,
+  `unsafe-inline`, or `unsafe-eval` exception is present.
+- Trusted Types blocks DOM injection sinks in supporting browsers, with no
+  permitted policy factories. There is no JavaScript requiring an exception.
+- `X-Frame-Options: DENY` also covers browsers without CSP frame protection.
+- `nosniff`, `no-referrer`, and Permissions Policy limit MIME confusion,
+  referrer disclosure, and access to unused sensitive device features.
+- The obsolete XSS filter is disabled with `X-XSS-Protection: 0`.
+- Headers apply to HTML, CSS, logout, and error responses.
+
+## Deployment and policy rollout
+
+Use `CSP_MODE=report-only` to evaluate the policy without CSP blocking, then set
+`CSP_MODE=enforce` (the default) to enforce it. Invalid modes stop startup rather
+than silently disabling protection. Report-only produces browser diagnostics;
+there is no report collector or persistent telemetry. Configure a trusted CSP
+reporting service and its reporting directives if centralized monitoring is
+needed before rollout. X-Frame-Options and the other independent headers remain
+active in report-only mode.
+
+Set `ENABLE_HSTS=1` only once the public deployment is HTTPS-only. It adds
+`Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000`; subdomains and preload are
+deliberately excluded. TLS termination and HTTP-to-HTTPS redirects belong at the
+deployment edge. Do not trust arbitrary forwarded protocol headers to enable
+HSTS. Verify the hosting proxy preserves these headers; serving the HTML directly
+from a different static host requires configuring equivalent response headers
+there. A meta CSP cannot supply report-only or frame-ancestors protections.
+
+For future features, allow only required resources and use safe DOM APIs such as
+`textContent` for untrusted text. CSP supplements input handling; it does not
+replace it. `/logout` is currently only a static message: real authentication
+would require server-side sessions and CSRF protection for state-changing actions.
+
+## Verification
+
+`npm test` checks handler responses with in-memory request/response objects,
+resource compatibility, restrictive directives, rollout configuration, error
+headers, and filesystem isolation. `npm run build` checks JavaScript syntax.
+Neither command starts a server or imports `server.js`. No browser verification
+was performed; actual browser enforcement and deployed proxy behavior remain
+unverified.
+
+References: [MDN CSP deployment guidance](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Practical_implementation_guides/CSP),
+[OWASP security headers](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTTP_Headers_Cheat_Sheet.html),
+[OWASP DOM XSS prevention](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html).
